For binary detection, each window is assigned a label $y \in \{0,1\}$ derived from an \texttt{is\_attack} indicator. In addition, the dataset includes structured metadata for richer analyses, including attack onset index within the window, motor type (ACIM/PMSM), phase targeting, severity level(s), and operating-point context (speed/load). This structure supports real-time binary detection and condition-aware studies such as motor, phase, severity and onset tasks. 

Table.~\ref{tab:dataset_schema} summarizes the input feature groups used to construct X and the associated output labels and contextual metadata used in the dataset.
The learning task is to estimate $\hat{y}=f_{\theta}(X)$ from these labeled windows. Normal and attack feature windows are compared in Fig.~\ref{fig:signal_windows}; examples of phase, frequency, and THD time-series variation are provided in Fig.~\ref{fig:feature_timeseries}.

\begin{figure}[!t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/signals_apec2027.pdf}
    \caption{Normal vs Cyber-attack data}
    \label{fig:signal_windows}
\end{figure}
The label vector \texttt{y} provides extensive metadata to support both binary and multi-class research objectives. Its 13 columns include flags for \texttt{is\_attack} (set to 1 if any sample in the window is an attack), \texttt{is\_onset} (the index of the first attack sample in the window), \texttt{attack\_id}, \texttt{attack\_type}, and the targeted \texttt{Motor} (encoded as 1 for ACIM, 2 for PMSM, and 0 for None). Additional columns detail the specific phase targeted (A, B, C), the corresponding severity levels, and any speed or load alterations. The attack and phase flags are max-pooled over each window; the onset label records the first attack index. Normal phase labels are $(A,B,C)=(0,0,0)$, and ROCOF denotes rate of change of frequency. Stored severity labels are evaluated in dataset units, separately from the physical injection parameter $\alpha$.

